.TH SPARSE 2
.SH NAME
sparse: newfrompattern, clear, get, set, add, matvec, dirichlet \- a compressed sparse row matrix
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sparse.m";
sparse := load Sparse Sparse->PATH;
CSR: import sparse;

m := sparse->newfrompattern(n, elems);
sparse->add(m, row, col, value);
y := sparse->matvec(m, x);
sparse->dirichlet(m, b, rows, values);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sparse
is a square compressed-sparse-row (CSR) matrix: a
.B CSR
holds
.IR n ,
.BR rowptr " (length " n +1),
.BR colidx " and " val " (both length " nnz ),
each row's slice of
.B colidx
/
.B val
sorted by column. This (not a stencil closure) is what makes
.IR fem (2)
a genuine sparse-matrix assembly: its
.IR krylov (2)
.B Apply
for a
.I Sparse
matrix is this matrix's own
.BR matvec ,
applied to real assembled values, never a stencil evaluated on the fly
the way
.IR pde (2)'s
matrix-free implicit solvers are.
.PP
.B Matvec
computes the product through
.IR math (2)'s
.B spmv
builtin, which is nine to ten times faster than the Limbo loop it
replaced and is where a Krylov solve spends nearly all its time.
The row loop and its summation order are unchanged, so results differ
only by the compiler contracting a multiply and an add into a fused
one \(em about two units in the last place.
It falls back to the Limbo loop if
.IR math (2)
cannot be loaded, so this is a speed change and not a new dependency.
Each call returns a fresh array, as
.IR krylov (2)
requires of any
.BR Apply .
.PP
.B Newfrompattern
builds the symbolic pattern once from
.IR elems ,
a per-element list of the global node ids it touches (e.g.
.IR femesh (2)'s
own
.BR elemnodes ),
before any numeric assembly \(em every pair of nodes sharing an
element becomes a nonzero, including the diagonal. A real FE/sparse
code never rebuilds the pattern per solve, only refills the numeric
values via repeated
.B clear
/
.B add
calls, which is why the pattern and the values are built in two
separate steps here.
.PP
.BR Clear ,
.BR get ,
.BR set ,
and
.B add
read or write one entry;
.B get
returns 0.0 for a
.RI ( row , col )
pair outside the pattern rather than an error, since that's a genuine
zero, not a mistake.
.B Matvec
computes
.IR m * x .
.PP
.B Dirichlet
eliminates a set of fixed degrees of freedom from
.RI ( m , b )
in place: for each
.RI ( rows [ i ], values [ i ]),
it zeros that row and column of
.I m
(folding each column's contribution into
.IR b ),
sets the diagonal to 1, and sets
.IR b [ rows [ i ]]
to
.IR values [ i ].
This keeps
.I m
symmetric positive definite when it started that way \(em unlike just
overwriting the row, which drops symmetry and
.IR krylov (2)'s
.B cg
convergence guarantee with it.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/sparse.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/sparse.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR femesh (2),
.IR krylov (2),
.IR fem (2)
